                "headline": "Flash Flood Warning issued September 12 at 4:14PM MST until September 12 at 7:15PM MST by NWS Flagstaff AZ",
                "description": "FFWFGZ\n\nThe National Weather Service in Flagstaff has issued a\n\n* Flash Flood Warning for...\nYavapai County in west central Arizona...\n\n* Until 715 PM MST.\n\n* At 414 PM MST, Doppler radar indicated thunderstorms producing\nheavy rain across the warned area, including the Dewey region and\nalong the Agua Fria River drainage south of Dewey. Between 0.75 and\n1.75 inches of rain have fallen. Additional rainfall amounts of\n0.25 to 0.75 inches are possible in the warned area. Flash flooding\nis ongoing or expected to begin shortly.\n\nHAZARD...Life threatening flash flooding. Thunderstorms producing\nflash flooding.\n\nSOURCE...Radar indicated.\n\nIMPACT...Life threatening flash flooding of creeks and streams,\nnormally dry washes, urban areas, highways, streets and\nunderpasses.\n\n* Some locations that will experience flash flooding include...\nDewey.\n\nThis includes the following highways...\nState Route 69 between mile markers 278 and 284. Interstate 17 near\nmile marker 266.\n\nThis includes the following creeks, washes and rivers...\nAgua Fria River, Little Hackberry Wash, Ash Creek, Coyote Wash,\nLynx Creek, Big Bug Creek, Osborne Spring Wash, Brushy Wash,\nChaparral Gulch and Yarber Wash.",
                "instruction": "Flooding of washes and creeks will occur, some dirt roads will become\nmuddy and impassable. Paved roads and underpasses could become\nflooded as well.\n\nLow water crossings will become dangerous in heavy rain. Do not\nattempt to cross flooded roads. Find an alternate route.",
